= Cognition — Technical =

== Explanatory levels ==
Cognitive explanations can be stated at multiple levels:

<pre>
task / computational problem
        ↓
representations + algorithms
        ↓
physical / neural implementation
</pre>

This resembles Marr's influential distinction but should not be treated as the only valid framework.

== Architectures ==
Competing cognitive architectures include symbolic, connectionist, Bayesian/probabilistic, dynamical, predictive-processing, embodied, enactive, and hybrid approaches.

They differ over what counts as a representation, how learning occurs, and how cognition relates to perception and action.

== Bounded processing ==
Human cognition operates under constraints in memory, attention, time, knowledge, and control.

These constraints motivate research on heuristics, [[Cognitive load|cognitive load]], bounded rationality, and adaptive strategy selection.

== Measurement ==
Behavioral accuracy, response time, eye movement, psychophysics, neuroimaging, electrophysiology, computational modeling, lesion studies, and process tracing measure different projections of cognitive systems.

No single dependent variable is cognition itself.

